<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-gay-rights">
  <title>Results from surveys on gay rights</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"Surveys show that surveys never lie."</p>
      <p>--- Natalie Angier</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      Attitudes to same-sex marriage have been surveyed over a number of years. The survey discussed here was
      part of Annenberg's 2004 election year survey in the United States.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> How did people's opinions on same-sex marriage vary with age? How did the opinions vary
      across the country? Were there differences between opinions on a law at state level and opinions on a
      Constitutional Amendment at federal level?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> The Annenberg Public Policy Center of the University of Pennsylvania.</p>
    <p><em>Structure.</em> There were 81,422 people surveyed and 84 variables.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-gay-rights-age-sex-race">
    <title>Survey results by age, sex, and race</title>

    <p>
      Annenberg carried out rolling cross-section surveys for the US presidential elections of 2000, 2004, and
      2008. Interviews for the 2004 National Annenberg Election survey were carried out between October 2003 and
      November 2004. Questions were asked on a range of topics and the dataset includes responses to many, but not
      all. Only a few questions are studied here.
    </p>
    <p>
      A third of respondents were asked if they would favour or oppose a law allowing same-sex marriage in their
      state, while all respondents were asked if they were in favour of or opposed to introducing a Constitutional
      Amendment making same-sex marriage illegal.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-gays">
      <image source="images/gayS-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Support for same-sex marriage at state level by respondent age. A red dotted line marks 50% support.
          Cases with any values missing have been excluded and data for ages over 80 combined into one value at 81.
          Point areas are proportional to the number of respondents of that age.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      There is a fairly steady decline in support by age, starting with a few young ages with majority support for
      same-sex marriage among those expressing an opinion. With increasing age it looks like a step function with
      about the same level of support between ages 40 and 55 and similarly between ages 62 and 77.
    </p>
    <p>
      The dataset is large enough to look at opinions by age and sex together. Females are more in favour than
      males at almost every age. The effect of sex and race together can also be investigated with a doubledecker
      plot. Patterns of support by race are similar for males and females, with female rates being higher and rates
      for Black respondents lowest.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-gaysg">
      <image source="images/gaySg-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Support for same-sex marriage at state level by age of respondent for females (purple) and males (green).</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-gaygr">
      <image source="images/gayGR-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Support for same-sex marriage at state level by sex and race.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-gay-rights-map">
    <title>Mapping the survey results</title>

    <p>
      Rates of support vary substantially by state. A choropleth map of support for a state law permitting same-sex
      marriage shows no information for Alaska and Hawaii because the survey was restricted to the 48 continental
      states and Washington, DC.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-gaymap">
      <image source="images/gayMap-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Support for same-sex marriage at state level on a continuous colour scale from 10% to 70%.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Some regional patterns are apparent, such as higher support in New England and on the West Coast. As always
      with choropleth maps, area does not reflect population. There are several larger states with small populations
      and smaller states with larger populations.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-gaycis">
      <image source="images/gayCIs-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Support for same-sex marriage by state with 95% confidence intervals, states grouped by region and ordered
          within region by estimated population proportion in favour.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-mapreg">
      <image source="images/mapReg-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>The four US regions defined by the Census Bureau.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-gaymapc">
      <image source="images/gayMapC-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Support for same-sex marriage at state level using a population cartogram.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Cartograms can help address the population issue, using number of respondents as a substitute for state
      population. The pattern suggests three broad groups: East, West, and centre.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-gay-rights-questions">
    <title>Comparing responses to related, but different questions</title>

    <introduction>

    <p>
      Another question asked whether respondents were in favour of or opposed to introducing a Constitutional
      Amendment making same-sex marriage illegal. All respondents were asked and the support pattern with age differs
      substantially from the state-law question.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-gayf">
      <image source="images/gayF-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Scatterplot of opposition to a Constitutional Amendment making same-sex marriage illegal by age of
          respondent. Cases with missing values are excluded; ages above 80 are combined into one value at 81.
          Point areas are proportional to the number of respondents of that age.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      While there was a majority against same-sex marriage at state level for most ages except the youngest, there
      was a majority against a Constitutional Amendment at almost every age, with little age variation.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-gayb">
      <image source="images/gayB-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Line plots of support for same-sex marriage at federal level (left) and opposition to a Constitutional
          Amendment (right) by age of respondent for males (green) and females (purple).
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Differences between graphics depend strongly on exact question wording. For the state question, "in favour"
      means in favour of same-sex marriage. For the federal question, "in favour" means in favour of an Amendment
      banning same-sex marriage, and respondents were offered an additional "Neither favor nor oppose" option.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-gayfsbars">
      <image source="images/gayFSbars-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Barcharts of the full responses to the two questions.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Most people with opinions on the issues have strong opinions. The two question framings were important.
      Those not expressing an opinion are shown in grey, and the vertical scales differ because only one third were
      asked the state question.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-gayfs">
      <image source="images/gayFS-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Multiple barcharts of responses to support for same-sex marriage at state level by responses to support
          for a Constitutional Amendment banning it.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      A key feature is respondents strongly opposing same-sex marriage in their own state while also strongly
      opposing a Constitutional Amendment banning same-sex marriage. This combination drives much of the difference
      between the two-question comparison graphics.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-gaybystate">
      <image source="images/gayByState-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Scatterplot of responses to the two same-sex marriage questions by state (point areas proportional to the
          number of respondents in each state).
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Opposition to a Constitutional Amendment making same-sex marriage illegal was much stronger than support for a
      state law permitting same-sex marriage in all states. The two responses were highly correlated across states,
      with the federal-rate increase accompanied by a larger increase in the state-rate measure.
    </p>

    <p>
      Opinions on gay rights are complex and several additional related questions were asked in the Annenberg survey.
      The survey is nearly twenty years old; more recent surveys suggest the US population has become more favourable
      toward same-sex marriage.
    </p>

    <p>
      <em>Answers.</em> Older people were more against a same-sex marriage law in their state. Women were more in
      favour than men at all ages. There was a majority at all ages against a Constitutional Amendment banning
      same-sex marriage.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Further questions.</em> Respondents were asked additional related questions: how did those responses relate
      to same-sex marriage responses? What results do more recent surveys show? How do opinions vary on other
      contentious issues in the survey?
    </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-gay-rights-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Bubble charts show how much evidence underlies a particular point.</p></li>
        <li><p>Doubledecker plots show both group rates and group sizes.</p></li>
        <li><p>For many categories, it helps to group first and then order by value.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
